-- title:
--   Constant fibre rank gives a basis after inverting one element
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring that is an integral domain, with underlying type in universe $u$, and let $P$ be a finitely generated $A$-module (an additive commutative group with an $A$-module structure satisfying `Module.Finite A P`), with underlying type in universe $v$. Fix a natural number $d$ and a finite set $S$ of ideals of $A$ such that every $\mathfrak p \in S$ is maximal and $S$ is nonempty. Assume that for every type $K$ in the same universe $u$ as $A$, carrying a field structure and an $A$-algebra structure, the $K$-vector space $K \otimes_A P$ has $\operatorname{finrank}_K (K \otimes_A P) = d$; that is, all fibres of $P$ at fields receiving $A$ have rank exactly $d$. The conclusion asserts the existence of an element $f \in A$ and a family $e : \mathrm{Fin}\, d \to P$ of $d$ elements of $P$ such that: $f \notin \mathfrak p$ for every $\mathfrak p \in S$; the family $e$ is linearly independent over $A$; and for every $p \in P$ one has $f \cdot p \in \operatorname{span}_A(\operatorname{range} e)$, i.e. $f P$ is contained in the $A$-submodule generated by $e_0, \dots, e_{d-1}$.
--
--   This is the simultaneous local freeness statement for a finite module of constant fibre rank: after inverting a single element $f$ not lying in any of the given finitely many maximal ideals, the elements $e_0,\dots,e_{d-1}$ of $P$ form a basis of $P[1/f]$ over $A[1/f]$, so the basic open set $D(f) \subseteq \operatorname{Spec} A$ contains the prescribed closed points and $P$ becomes free on it. It is used in the construction of refinements of a two-affine open cover of a scheme on which a pushforward module admits a basis.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Module_exists_forall_notMem_and_linearIndependent_and_smul_mem_span_of_finrank_baseChange_eq.lean

